`fs.rm` and `fs.promises.rm` do not follow symlinks by default in either version. They remove the link itself, not the target. I know of no release note in the v21 or v22 changelog that changes this default.

To confirm before you upgrade:

1. Check `https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/doc/changelogs/CHANGELOG_V22.md` for `fs.rm` or `fs.promises.rm` entries.
2. Run this on both Node versions and compare the output:
```js
const fs = require('fs/promises');
const os = require('os');
const path = require('path');

async function test() {
  const dir = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'rmtest-'));
  const target = path.join(dir, 'target.txt');
  const link = path.join(dir, 'link');
  await fs.writeFile(target, 'data');
  await fs.symlink(target, link);
  await fs.rm(link);
  console.log('link removed:', !(await fs.access(link).then(() => true).catch(() => false)));
  console.log('target still exists:', await fs.access(target).then(() => true).catch(() => false));
}
test();
```

If the target survives on both versions, behavior is unchanged for your case.

